AKS Backup & Restore — Operations Runbook
Platform: BFH AKS / IHUB  |  Environments: dev → prod  |  Terraform modules: terraform/modules/main/backup.tf, workload-onboarding/

Golden Path
1.  Configure conf.yaml (cluster, storage, vault, policy, instance)
2.  Terraform provisions AKS + backup infrastructure
3.  Backup Policy runs automatically every 4 hours
4.  Recovery point becomes available in the Backup Vault
5.  Operator initiates restore (Azure Portal / CLI — not Terraform)
6.  Restore cluster receives namespaces, service accounts, workloads
7.  Workload onboarding pipeline runs for the ORIGINAL cluster
8.  Pipeline discovers the *-rst-* cluster automatically from conf.yaml
9.  Additional Federated Identity Credential (FIC) is created — additive only
10. Restored pods authenticate via Workload Identity and are validated

	[!TIP]
	Key design principle: Restore-cluster Workload Identity is additive. The original federated credential on the source cluster is never modified, replaced, or removed.

Table of Contents
	1. Executive Overview
	2. Complete End-to-End Flow
	3. Terraform Code Structure — Backup Infrastructure
	4. Prerequisites
	5. Deployment
	6. Running Backups
	7. Restore Operations
	8. Workload Identity Architecture for Restore Clusters
	9. Operational Checklists
	10. Troubleshooting Decision Trees
	11. Known Issues & Lessons Learned

1. Executive Overview
Every AKS cluster in the IHUB platform is protected using Azure Backup for AKS (the Data Protection service), configured declaratively through the same Terraform that provisions the cluster itself. Adding backup coverage to a new cluster is a conf.yaml change, not a manual Portal setup.
flowchart TD
    A[Original AKS Cluster] --> B[Data Protection Extension]
    B --> C[Backup Vault]
    C --> D[Recovery Point]
    D --> E[Restore Cluster]
    E --> F[Workload Identity]
    F --> G[Azure Entra ID]
    G --> H[Azure Resources]
	[!IMPORTANT]
	Restore is not a Terraform resource. Terraform provisions the infrastructure and identity configuration required for restore validation; the restore job itself is triggered through Azure Portal or CLI.
Five things a new engineer needs to understand:
#	Concept	Summary
1	How backups are created	AKS → Data Protection Extension → Backup Vault → Recovery Point
2	How restore works	Recovery Point → Restore Cluster (Portal/CLI action)
3	What Terraform owns	Infrastructure + Backup configuration + Workload Identity configuration
4	What the operator owns	Selecting recovery point, initiating restore, monitoring, validation
5	Why the additional FIC exists	Same Kubernetes subject + different OIDC issuer = additional federated credential

2. Complete End-to-End Flow
flowchart TD
    A[conf.yaml] --> B[Terraform]
    B --> C[AKS + Backup Infrastructure]
    C --> D[Backup Policy]
    D --> E[Backup Instance]
    E --> F["Scheduled Backup (every 4h)"]
    F --> G[Recovery Point]
    G --> H[Operator Initiates Restore]
    H --> I[Restore Cluster]
    I --> J[Workload Onboarding Pipeline]
    J --> K[Additional Federated Credential]
    K --> L[Azure Authentication]
    L --> M[Application Validation]
This is the single flow that ties every section of this runbook together: sections 3–6 cover steps 1–6 (build and run backups), section 7 covers steps 6–8 (restore), and section 8 covers steps 7–10 (the identity mechanism that makes restored workloads actually work).

3. Terraform Code Structure — Backup Infrastructure
Everything lives in one flat module, bfhaksplatform/terraform/modules/main/backup.tf, driven entirely by the instance's conf.yaml. There is no dedicated variables.tf for backup — the inputs are YAML blocks decoded once into local.conf.
Backup architecture
flowchart TD
    subgraph CLUSTER["AKS Cluster"]
        K8S[Kubernetes Resources]
        WL[Workloads]
        EXT[Backup Extension]
    end
    EXT --> VAULT[Backup Vault]
    VAULT --> POLICY[Backup Policy]
    POLICY --> INSTANCE[Backup Instance]
    INSTANCE --> RP[Recovery Points]
Resource definitions
Resource	What it does
azurerm_storage_account	ZRS, Cool-tier blob storage per storage_account entry — the backup data landing zone. Public network access disabled, TLS 1.2 minimum, versioning + 7-day soft delete.
azurerm_storage_container	Private blob container inside the storage account.
azurerm_kubernetes_cluster_extension	Installs microsoft.dataprotection.kubernetes into the target cluster, pointed at the storage account/container.
azurerm_data_protection_backup_vault	Zone-redundant vault, soft delete always-on, immutability locked, system-assigned identity.
azurerm_management_lock	CanNotDelete lock applied to every vault.
azurerm_data_protection_backup_policy_kubernetes_cluster	Defines schedule (every 4 hours) and retention. Named bp-<policy-name>.
azurerm_data_protection_backup_instance_kubernetes_cluster	Binds cluster + vault + policy. Named bi-<cluster-name>. Cluster-scoped, no namespace/type filters by default.
azurerm_kubernetes_cluster_trusted_access_role_binding	Grants the vault trusted access into the cluster with the backup-operator role.
azurerm_role_assignment ×7	RBAC for the vault identity, the extension identity, and the cluster's own identity.
Terraform dependency graph
flowchart TD
    AKS[AKS Cluster Module] --> EXT[Backup Extension]
    SA[Storage Account] --> SC[Storage Container]
    SC --> EXT
    EXT --> INSTANCE[Backup Instance]
    VAULT[Backup Vault] --> LOCK[Management Lock]
    VAULT --> POLICY[Backup Policy]
    POLICY --> INSTANCE
    AKS --> INSTANCE
    VAULT --> INSTANCE
    INSTANCE --> TRUST[Trusted Access Role Binding]
    VAULT --> RBAC["RBAC Role Assignments (x7)"]
    INSTANCE --> RBAC
	[!NOTE]
	The AKS cluster module and its managed identities must already exist in the same Terraform graph — the backup module reads data.azurerm_user_assigned_identity.cluster_identity directly off the cluster's own conf entry.
Key code — backup policy & instance
The policy defines the 4-hour schedule and retention; the instance is what actually binds a specific cluster to that policy and vault:
resource "azurerm_data_protection_backup_policy_kubernetes_cluster" "aks_policy" {
  for_each             = local.backup_policies
  name                 = "bp-${each.key}"
  resource_group_name  = local.conf.settings.resource_group_name
  vault_name           = each.value.vault_name
# every 4 hours
  backup_repeating_time_intervals = ["R/2026-01-01T00:00:00+00:00/PT4H"]
default_retention_rule {
    life_cycle {
      duration        = "P${each.value.retention_days}D"
      data_store_type = "OperationalStore"
    }
  }
}
resource "azurerm_data_protection_backup_instance_kubernetes_cluster" "aks_backup_instance" {
  for_each                      = local.backup_instances
  name                          = "bi-${each.key}"
  vault_id                      = azurerm_data_protection_backup_vault.backup_vault[each.value.backup_vault].id
  kubernetes_cluster_id         = module.aks_cluster[each.key].id
  snapshot_resource_group_name  = data.azurerm_resource_group.snapshot_rg.name
  backup_policy_id              = azurerm_data_protection_backup_policy_kubernetes_cluster.aks_policy[each.value.backup_policy].id
backup_datasource_parameters {
    cluster_scoped_resources_enabled = true
    included_namespaces              = []
    excluded_namespaces              = []
  }
}
Inputs & outputs
No dedicated variables — every input comes from these conf.yaml blocks, cross-referenced by name:
conf.yaml block	Referenced by name from
storage_account	—
aks_backup_extension	aks_cluster + storage_account
backup_vault	—
backup_policy	backup_vault
backup_instance	aks_cluster + backup_policy
No dedicated outputs — this is a leaf module consumed only by the platform instance pipeline.

4. Prerequisites
Permissions / RBAC
	• Owner, or Contributor + User Access Administrator, at the resource group scope — plain Contributor cannot create the role assignments this module manages.
	• OIDC federation configured for the pipeline's service principal (ARM_USE_OIDC, ARM_CLIENT_ID, ARM_TENANT_ID).
Azure resources that must already exist
	• The target AKS cluster and its cluster/kubelet managed identities.
	• The snapshot resource group — the instance's own settings.resource_group_name.
	• tenant_id and subscription_id set under settings in conf.yaml.
Terraform state
	• Backup resources live in the same main TFC workspace as the rest of the platform instance — no separate backup-only workspace.
conf.yaml entries required:
storage_account:
  - name: sasbfhaksihubdevbackup
    container: blobaksdevbackup
backup_vault:
  - name: bv-bfhaks-ihub-eus2-dev-01
aks_backup_extension:
  - aks_cluster: aks-bfhaks-ihub-eus2-dev-01
    backup_vault: bv-bfhaks-ihub-eus2-dev-01
    storage_account: sasbfhaksihubdevbackup
    storage_container: blobaksdevbackup
backup_policy:
  - name: aks-4h-backup-7d-retention
    backup_vault: bv-bfhaks-ihub-eus2-dev-01
backup_instance:
  - aks_cluster: aks-bfhaks-ihub-eus2-dev-01
    backup_policy: aks-4h-backup-7d-retention

5. Deployment
	1. Update conf.yaml — add/edit the storage_account, aks_backup_extension, backup_vault, backup_policy, and backup_instance blocks.
	2. Run the platform pipeline (or plan locally) against the instance's main TFC workspace.
	3. Review the plan — first run expects creates for the storage account/container, cluster extension, backup vault + lock, policy, instance, trusted access binding, and all 7 role assignments.
	4. Apply.
Validating the deployment:
	• Extension health: kubectl get pods -n dataprotection-microsoft — pods should be Running.
	• Azure Portal: Backup center → the vault should list the new Backup Instance with a healthy status.
Common issues:
	[!WARNING]
	RBAC propagation delay — backup instance creation can fail validation moments after role assignments are created, since Azure AD propagation isn't instant. Re-run apply if this happens on the first pass.
	[!WARNING]
	Extension stuck provisioning — usually a network path issue. The storage account has public_network_access_enabled = false; confirm the cluster's outbound path can reach it.
	[!WARNING]
	Can't change vault settings — the CanNotDelete lock also blocks some vault-level immutable setting changes. Remove the lock first, then reapply.

6. Running Backups
Backups are not triggered manually day to day — the policy runs on its own schedule.
Backup configuration
Component	Configuration
Schedule	Every 4 hours
Non-Prod Retention	7 days
Prod Retention	90 days
Scope	Cluster scoped
Extension	microsoft.dataprotection.kubernetes
Triggering a manual backup:
az dataprotection backup-instance adhoc-backup \
  --resource-group <resource-group> \
  --vault-name bv-bfhaks-ihub-eus2-dev-01 \
  --backup-instance-name bi-aks-bfhaks-ihub-eus2-dev-01 \
  --rule-name Default
Or in the Portal: Backup center → select instance → Backup now.
Verifying completion:
	• Portal: Backup center → vault → Backup Instances → select instance → Jobs tab.
	• CLI: az dataprotection job list --resource-group <rg> --vault-name <vault>
Checking status in the Portal: search "Backup center" → Overview gives a vault-level summary across all protected instances, last backup status, and any failures.

7. Restore Operations
	[!IMPORTANT]
	Restore is not a Terraform resource. There is no restore block in this codebase — restore is triggered operationally (Portal or CLI). Terraform's role is provisioning the target cluster and wiring up workload identity for it afterward.
Restore architecture
flowchart TD
    SRC[Source Cluster] --> RP[Recovery Point]
    RP --> RESTORE[Azure Backup Restore]
    RESTORE --> TGT["Restore Cluster (separate from source)"]
    TGT --> NS[Namespaces]
    NS --> SVC[Service Accounts]
    SVC --> WL[Workloads]
Restore responsibility
Activity	Terraform	Operator
Create AKS	Yes	No
Configure Backup	Yes	No
Create Backup Policy	Yes	No
Create Backup Instance	Yes	No
Trigger Restore	No	Yes
Select Recovery Point	No	Yes
Validate Restore	No	Yes
Configure Additional FIC	Yes	No
Restore procedure
	1. Open the recovery point — Backup center → vault → Backup Instance for the source cluster → Restore → pick the recovery point by timestamp.
	2. Choose the target — restore in place on the same cluster, or (for validation) into a separate restore/validation cluster. The target must already be onboarded in conf.yaml's aks_cluster list with a name matching *-rst-* (e.g. aks-bfhaks-ihub-eus2-dev-rst-01).
	3. Choose scope — cluster-scoped resources, specific namespaces, or resource types/label selectors.
	4. Run the restore job — namespaces and their Kubernetes objects, including service accounts, land on the target cluster with identical names to the source.
	5. Run the workload onboarding pipeline for the original cluster — not the restore cluster. Full mechanics in section 8.
Restore sequence
sequenceDiagram
    participant OP as Operator
    participant AB as Azure Backup
    participant SRC as Source AKS (dev-01)
    participant RST as Restore AKS (dev-rst-01)
    participant WO as Workload Onboarding
    participant AAD as Azure Entra ID
    participant POD as Restored Pod
OP->>AB: Select recovery point
    AB->>RST: Restore Kubernetes resources
    RST-->>RST: Namespaces + Service Accounts appear
    WO->>SRC: Run pipeline for original cluster
    WO->>WO: Discover *-rst-* cluster in conf.yaml
    WO->>RST: Query OIDC issuer
    WO->>AAD: Create additional Federated Credential
    POD->>AAD: Request Azure AD token (subject + issuer B)
    AAD->>AAD: Validate issuer + subject against FIC
    AAD-->>POD: Issue token
    POD->>AAD: Access Azure resource with token
Validating restored data
	• kubectl get pods -n <namespace> --context <restore-cluster> — pods should reach Running.
	• Check pod logs for successful Azure token exchange — an AADSTS700211 error means the federated credential is missing or the issuer doesn't match.
	• Confirm application-level data and state match what's expected for that recovery point.
Rollback
Because validation restores target a separate cluster by convention, there's normally nothing to roll back on the original — if validation fails, decommission or scale down the restore cluster.
If a restore was performed in place on the original cluster (not recommended), rollback means re-running the restore job against an earlier known-good recovery point, or restoring individual namespace resources from a prior backup job rather than the whole cluster.

8. Workload Identity Architecture for Restore Clusters
This is the most important architectural concept in this document.
Why an additional federated credential — not a replacement
Azure Backup restores the Kubernetes namespace and service account onto the restore cluster with identical names to the source. The restored pod's identity subject is therefore exactly the same on both clusters — only the OIDC issuer differs, because each AKS cluster has its own.
flowchart TD
    SUBJ["Same Subject: system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;sa-name&gt;"]
    SUBJ --> D01[dev-01]
    SUBJ --> DRST[dev-rst-01]
    D01 --> ISSA[OIDC Issuer A]
    DRST --> ISSB[OIDC Issuer B]
    ISSA --> FIC1["Federated Credential #1 (original)"]
    ISSB --> FIC2["Federated Credential #2 (additional)"]
    FIC1 --> MI[Same Managed Identity]
    FIC2 --> MI
    MI --> RES[Azure Resources]
	[!TIP]
	Key design principle: the subject stays the same. The OIDC issuer changes. Therefore the correct fix is an additional federated credential, not a replacement of the original.
	[!CAUTION]
	Never process a *-rst-* cluster as an independent Phase 2 workload-onboarding target when it shares the original workload identity model.
Before — broken design
Early implementations processed the restore cluster as its own independent cluster in the pipeline, sometimes sharing the same Terraform workspace/state as the original to avoid provisioning a new one. Kubernetes namespace, service account, and federated credential resources all resolve to the same Terraform resource address regardless of which cluster is being processed — so Terraform interpreted the two clusters as one resource repeatedly changing target.
flowchart TD
    D1[dev-01] --> SHARED["Same Phase 2 Workspace / State"]
    D2[dev-rst-01] --> SHARED
    SHARED --> ADDR[Same Terraform Resource Address]
    ADDR --> NS[Namespace]
    ADDR --> SA[Service Account]
    ADDR --> FIC[Federated Credential]
    NS --> REDIR[Resources redirected / replaced]
    SA --> REDIR
    FIC --> REDIR
    REDIR --> IMPACT[Original cluster impacted]
After — correct design
The restore cluster is never looped over as its own Phase 2 target. The original cluster's run is completely unaffected; the restore cluster's federated credential is added as an extra resource within that same run.
flowchart TD
    D01[dev-01] --> STATE["dev-01's Own Phase 2 State"]
    STATE --> NS["Namespace (unchanged)"]
    STATE --> SA["Service Account (unchanged)"]
    STATE --> FIC1["Federated Credential #1 (unchanged)"]
DRST[dev-rst-01] --> BKP[Azure Backup Restore]
    BKP --> ENS["Existing Namespace (restored)"]
    BKP --> ESA["Existing Service Account (restored)"]
    ESA --> ISSB[OIDC Issuer B]
    ISSB --> FIC2["Federated Credential #2 (additive)"]
    FIC1 --> MI[Same Managed Identity]
    FIC2 --> MI
	[!WARNING]
	Terraform changes to federated credentials or RBAC can invalidate cached Managed Identity tokens on already-running pods. Restart affected workloads after such changes — see the MI Token Caching entry in section 11.
Code changes, by file
phases/01-per-instance/locals.tf — the cluster list used downstream now excludes RST clusters by regex, instead of a fragile hardcoded exclusion list:
locals {
  aks_clusters_all = [
    for k, v in data.azurerm_kubernetes_cluster.aks : { name = v.name, id = v.id }
  ]
# Non-RST clusters only
  aks_clusters = [
    for cluster in local.aks_clusters_all :
    cluster if !can(regex(".*-rst-.*", cluster.name))
  ]
}
phases/01-per-instance/outputs.tf — the cluster-name output consumed by the Phase 2 loop reflects that same filtered list:
output "aks_cluster_names" {
  value = [for cluster in local.aks_clusters : cluster.name]
}
cicd/deploy/phase2_terraform_runs.ps1 — a plain loop; every cluster (including any future restore cluster, should it ever need one) gets its own dedicated workspace, no special-case redirect:
foreach ($cluster_name in $aks_cluster_names) {
    $tfc_workspace_phase2 = $tfc_workspace_phase1 -replace "phase1$", "phase2-$cluster_name"
    $env:TF_WORKSPACE = $tfc_workspace_phase2
    $env:TF_VAR_bfhaks_instance_cluster_name = $cluster_name
    terraform init -no-color -input=false
    terraform apply -no-color -input=false -auto-approve
}
phases/02-per-instance-per-cluster/main.tf — restore-cluster detection is dynamic, read straight from conf.yaml instead of a hardcoded per-environment map:
locals {
  restore_target_cluster_names = [
    for c in local.bfhaks_instance_conf.aks_cluster : c.name
    if can(regex(".*-rst-.*", c.name))
  ]
restore_target = {
    for name in local.restore_target_cluster_names : name => {
      name                = name
      resource_group_name = local.bfhaks_instance_conf.settings.resource_group_name
    }
  }
}
data "azurerm_kubernetes_cluster" "additional_clusters" {
  for_each             = local.restore_target
  resource_group_name  = each.value.resource_group_name
  name                 = each.value.name
}
Passed into the module:
additional_cluster_oidc_issuer_urls = {
  for k, v in data.azurerm_kubernetes_cluster.additional_clusters : k => v.oidc_issuer_url
}
modules/kubernetes-resources/locals.tf and main.tf — RBAC for_each and the main federated credential's name/subject are plain, unconditional expressions, since this module now only ever processes the original cluster directly. The pre-existing workload_identity_additional resource handles the restore cluster's credential:
resource "azurerm_federated_identity_credential" "workload_identity_additional" {
  for_each = {
    for pair in flatten([
      for cluster_key, oidc_url in var.additional_cluster_oidc_issuer_urls : [
        for k, v in local.computed_component_map : {
          key = "${cluster_key}-${k}", cluster_key = cluster_key, oidc_url = oidc_url, component_key = k
        } if v.workload_identity == true
      ]
    ]) : pair.key => pair
  }
name      = "${kubernetes_service_account_v1.workload_identity[each.value.component_key].metadata.0.name}-${each.value.cluster_key}"
  parent_id = local.workload_identity_map[each.value.component_key].workload_identity.id
  audience  = ["api://AzureADTokenExchange"]
  issuer    = each.value.oidc_url
  subject   = local.federated_credential_subject[each.value.component_key]
lifecycle { ignore_changes = all }
}
modules/app_alerts/main.tf — alert rule group naming checks each cluster individually inside the loop, instead of a single module-level flag that couldn't distinguish clusters within the same batch:
name = "${type}-${name}-v${version}${can(regex(".*-rst-.*", each.value.cluster.name)) ? "-${each.value.cluster.name}" : ""}-AppAlerts"
Result
	Original cluster (dev-01)	Restore cluster (dev-rst-01)
Terraform workspace	Own, unchanged	Never provisioned — no separate run
Namespace / Service Account	Own, unchanged	Restored by Azure Backup, not Terraform
RBAC	Own, unchanged	Not created (not needed for federated auth)
Federated credential	sa-<workload>-v1, unchanged	sa-<workload>-v1-<rst-cluster-name>, added additively
Alert rule group	Plain name, unchanged	Suffixed name, no collision
This pattern is fully generic — any cluster matching *-rst-* in conf.yaml is picked up automatically, so the same logic covers sit-rst, uat-rst, etc. with zero additional code.

9. Operational Checklists
Pre-Deployment
	• [ ] conf.yaml updated with storage_account, aks_backup_extension, backup_vault, backup_policy, backup_instance
	• [ ] Target AKS cluster and its managed identities already exist
	• [ ] Pipeline service principal has Owner or Contributor + User Access Administrator on the resource group
	• [ ] TFC main workspace identified for the instance
Backup Validation
	• [ ] kubectl get pods -n dataprotection-microsoft shows extension pods Running
	• [ ] Backup Vault visible in Backup center with healthy status
	• [ ] Backup Instance shows at least one successful job
	• [ ] Retention matches expected value for environment (7d non-prod / 90d prod)
Pre-Restore
	• [ ] Recovery point confirmed
	• [ ] Restore target confirmed (in-place vs. *-rst-* validation cluster)
	• [ ] Target cluster healthy and reachable
	• [ ] Target cluster already listed in conf.yaml aks_cluster if it's a restore/validation cluster
	• [ ] Required identities and role assignments verified on source
Restore Execution
	• [ ] Recovery point selected in Backup center
	• [ ] Scope confirmed (cluster-scoped / namespace / resource type)
	• [ ] Restore job started and monitored to completion
	• [ ] Namespaces and service accounts confirmed present on target cluster
Post-Restore Validation
	• [ ] Workload onboarding pipeline run for the original cluster (not the restore cluster)
	• [ ] Additional federated credential confirmed created on the Managed Identity
	• [ ] kubectl get pods -n <namespace> --context <restore-cluster> shows pods Running
	• [ ] Pod logs show no AADSTS700211 errors
	• [ ] Application-level data/state validated against expected recovery point
Production Incident
	• [ ] Confirm whether the issue is backup-related or an unrelated application-level issue before escalating
	• [ ] Check original cluster's RBAC and federated credentials are unaffected
	• [ ] If Terraform recently touched federated credentials or RBAC, run kubectl rollout restart on affected workloads
	• [ ] Capture recovery point timestamp and job ID for the incident record
	• [ ] Document root cause and resolution in section 11 once closed

10. Troubleshooting Decision Trees
Backup failure
flowchart TD
    A[Backup failed] --> B[Check Backup Job in Portal]
    B --> C{Extension healthy?}
    C -->|No| D["Check dataprotection-microsoft pods"]
    C -->|Yes| E{RBAC issue?}
    E -->|Yes| F["Check vault / extension / snapshot RG role assignments"]
    E -->|No| G{Network issue?}
    G -->|Yes| H["Check private storage account connectivity"]
    G -->|No| I["Review Azure Backup job error details"]
Restore workload failure
flowchart TD
    A[Restored pod not healthy] --> B{Pod starting?}
    B -->|"No - ImagePullBackOff"| C["Check ACR federated credential + AcrPull"]
    B -->|"No - CrashLoop"| D{AADSTS700211 in logs?}
    D -->|Yes| E["OIDC issuer mismatch - check additional FIC"]
    D -->|No| F{CSI mount error?}
    F -->|Yes| G["Check Key Vault CSI permissions on target vault"]
    F -->|No| H{KEDA scaling error?}
    H -->|Yes| I["Check ScaledObject metric source in restore cluster"]
    H -->|No| J{Istio traffic error?}
    J -->|Yes| K["Check Istio revision matches restore cluster"]
    J -->|No| L[Proceed to application-level validation]
    B -->|"Yes, Running"| L

11. Known Issues & Lessons Learned
Issue	Symptom	Root Cause	Resolution	Prevention
Workload Identity OIDC Issuer Mismatch	AADSTS700211 on restored pods	Federated credential only trusted the original cluster's OIDC issuer	Pipeline detects the paired restore cluster automatically, queries its OIDC issuer, creates an additional federated credential trusting it	Always use the additive-FIC pattern (section 8) for any new restore cluster
KEDA ScaledObject Conflicts	ScaledObjects fail to scale in restore cluster	CRDs referenced metric sources that don't exist in the restore environment	Rebuilt ScaledObjects with the restore cluster's actual metric endpoints	Review KEDA metric source config as part of restore validation
CSI Secret Store Failures	Pods stuck on secret mount	Managed identities lacked Key Vault secret get/list permissions on the target vault	Granted proper secret permissions before restore, alongside RBAC/FIC work	Include Key Vault RBAC in the restore-cluster identity checklist
ACR Image Pull Permission Issues	ImagePullBackOff on restored pods	AcrPull role assignments didn't follow managed identities to the restore cluster	Ensured federated credentials existed for all workloads before restore	Create federated credentials before triggering restore, not after
Istio Revision Mismatches	Traffic routing failures via gateway/virtual service	Workload config pinned to DEV's Istio revision; restore cluster ran a different revision	Updated workload configs to reference the correct revision per environment	Validate Istio revision alignment as part of restore-cluster onboarding
Backup Infra Setup — UserErrorMissingMSIPermissionsOnSnapshotResourceGroup	Backup Terraform apply fails on first setup	Snapshot-creating identity lacked the correct role on the snapshot resource group	Resolved correct principal ID via data source and assigned proper RBAC role	Confirm all 7 RBAC role assignments in backup.tf apply cleanly before first backup run
Automount API Credentials Policy Blocking Backup Extension	Backup extension pods stuck in dataprotection-microsoft	Deny-effect Azure Policy blocked automounting API credentials cluster-wide, including the extension's own namespace	Added dataprotection-microsoft to excluded_namespaces in automount_api_credentials_disabled.tf	Keep system/extension namespaces in the policy's default exclusion list
Terraform State Drift — upgrade_override	Next Terraform run fails after manual Azure CLI change	Manual CLI flag changes on POC-01/POC-02 caused state to diverge from reality	Added upgrade_override to lifecycle { ignore_changes } in the shared AKS module	Avoid manual Azure CLI changes to Terraform-managed attributes; if unavoidable, add to ignore_changes
Grafana v11 Deprecation	Forced auto-upgrade risk after June 15, 2026	Standard SKU doesn't support v11 to begin with	Proactively upgraded to v12	Provision new Grafana instances on v12/v13 directly
Federated Identity Credential 409 Conflicts (Phase 2, dev-rst)	Conflict: Issuer and subject combination already exists	Terraform repeatedly tried to recreate FICs that already existed	Resolved via the additive-FIC redesign (section 8) plus lifecycle { ignore_changes = all }	Never recreate an existing FIC; always add additional ones instead
Harness Service Principal RBAC Gaps	Helm release history queries blocked during deployment	Rolebinding bound to the ServiceAccount only, not the service principal	Added the service principal under pipeline_identities with proper RBAC Writer role across environments	Verify pipeline identity RBAC when onboarding a new namespace
MI Token Caching After RBAC Changes (Sep 11 — Critical)	HTTP 500 on startup for api-transactions, api-statements, api-pricing	Pods cached Managed Identity tokens issued while permissions were briefly revoked during a Terraform run	Added kubectl rollout restart to the Harness pipeline immediately after Terraform completes	Always restart affected workloads after any deployment that touches federated credentials or RBAC
Configuration Drift Detection (Sep 4 vs. Present)	New feature deployments failing despite prior healthy state	Possible configuration drift or service principal rotation since last known-good state	Coordinated with DevOps team to validate and re-sync configuration	Periodically re-validate cluster configuration against known-good baselines
